The tag is a PDFium branch head, not a semantic version: upstream ships no
release tarballs and no tags (SP-1 F-1). `chromium/8076` therefore identifies
the branch the distributor built, and the SHA-256 below identifies the exact
bytes we fetched. Both are required; neither alone is a pin.

Artefacts
---------
Only platforms listed here may be fetched. fetch_pdfium.sh refuses any other
platform rather than downloading something it cannot verify.

  platform   asset                    size (bytes)  sha256
  ---------  -----------------------  ------------  ------
  mac-arm64  pdfium-mac-arm64.tgz          3487497  0d6781fe08906baff3d82c90953e519fbc4eb253fe76431e5ed53b157763b97c

  URL pattern:
    https://github.com/bblanchon/pdfium-binaries/releases/download/chromium/8076/<asset>

  Verify by hand:
    curl -fsSL -o a.tgz https://github.com/bblanchon/pdfium-binaries/releases/download/chromium/8076/pdfium-mac-arm64.tgz
    shasum -a 256 a.tgz

Only mac-arm64 is pinned because only mac-arm64 is built and tested by this
bootstrap. android-arm64 / ios-arm64 are deliberately absent: they are the
platforms SP-1's hermetic from-source build must produce, and adding a
prebuilt row for them here would make the bootstrap look like a mobile
solution. It is not one.

Build configuration of the fetched artefact
-------------------------------------------
Taken verbatim from `args.gn` inside the archive (also copied to
args.gn.mac-arm64 by the fetch script), and cross-checked against the arg set
SP-1 F-4 recorded as the known-good one:

    clang_use_chrome_plugins = false
    is_component_build       = false
    is_debug                 = false
    pdf_enable_v8            = false      <- matches SP-1 / T-105: no PDF JavaScript
    pdf_enable_xfa           = false      <- matches SP-1
    pdf_is_standalone        = true
    pdf_use_partition_alloc  = false      <- matches SP-1 F-3
    target_cpu               = "arm64"
    target_os                = "mac"
    treat_warnings_as_errors = false

`pdf_use_skia` is unset, i.e. the AGG rasteriser is what ships — which is what
file-format-support.md §7.8 asked for and what SP-1 F-4 predicted. Confirmed
independently by the presence of `licenses/agg23.txt` in the archive.

DEVIATION FROM THE TASK SHAPE, recorded rather than hidden
-----------------------------------------------------------
This release publishes **no static archive for macOS**. Every `pdfium-mac-*`
asset contains `lib/libpdfium.dylib` — a shared library — and there is no
`pdf_is_complete_lib = true` variant among the mac assets (checked against the
full asset list of chromium/8076). So the CMake target is an IMPORTED SHARED
library, not the IMPORTED STATIC one SP-1 §5 names. The *interface* is what
SP-1 cared about (an IMPORTED target consumed from our CMake) and that is
honoured; STATIC returns when the hermetic build produces the archive
ourselves. On a dev host the dylib's rpath is resolved by CMake's
BUILD_RPATH — nothing is installed system-wide.

